WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.180
In the first film, two phosphates on partner tails switched the receptor on.

2
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:13.200
Those receptors vary almost at random, so some recognise our own peptides.

3
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.520
What stops an armed T cell from attacking healthy tissue?

4
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:21.453
A fresh T cell does not trust signal one alone.

5
00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:28.253
Without a second cue, cultured T cells fall into anergy, a lasting unresponsive

6
00:00:28.253 --> 00:00:33.553
state. Full activation needs CD28 to grip B7.

7
00:00:34.893 --> 00:00:38.173
Presenting cells raise B7 when they sense infection.

8
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:42.697
Once activated, the T cell puts up CTLA-4.

9
00:00:43.697 --> 00:00:47.457
It resembles CD28 and binds the same B7.

10
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:49.817
But it is a brake.

11
00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:56.217
Each CTLA-4 dimer grips two B7 at once, about ten times more tightly.

12
00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:02.717
So CD28 is left empty, and it even drags B7 away to be destroyed.

13
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:06.707
A second brake, PD-1, works from inside.

14
00:01:08.047 --> 00:01:11.547
Many healthy cells and many tumours display PD-L1.

15
00:01:12.787 --> 00:01:17.347
When PD-L1 binds, two phosphates appear on PD-1's own tail.

16
00:01:18.607 --> 00:01:23.027
They call in SHP-2, an enzyme that strips phosphates off proteins.

17
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.947
So does it strip the two phosphates that called it in?

18
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.590
No, because those two phosphates are its anchor.

19
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.330
SHP-2 grips both with two SH2 domains, so it stays docked.

20
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:49.170
Its working end then swings sideways and erases activating phosphates on CD28 and

21
00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.210
partner tails. So this brake dims the cell from inside.

22
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:56.183
Here is the second core rule.

23
00:01:57.423 --> 00:02:01.903
Full activation needs a second signal, and checkpoints brake outside or inside.

24
00:02:03.243 --> 00:02:06.323
A checkpoint is just a brake a T cell must get past.

25
00:02:07.703 --> 00:02:12.823
Blocking antibodies cap CTLA-4 or PD-1 on the patient's T cells.

26
00:02:14.243 --> 00:02:18.403
Tumour-recognising T cells keep firing, though healthy tissue can inflame.

27
00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:23.747
Blocking a brake helps only if T cells already recognise the tumour.

28
00:02:25.047 --> 00:02:29.187
Otherwise, engineers build a chimeric antigen receptor, or CAR.

29
00:02:30.627 --> 00:02:34.567
An antibody tip outside is fused to the zeta tail inside.

30
00:02:35.907 --> 00:02:39.607
It grips surface proteins directly, so no MHC is needed.

31
00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:44.550
Yet a zeta tail alone gives signal one without signal two.

32
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.930
Those early cells often failed to persist in patients.

33
00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.810
Engineers spliced CD28 or 4-1BB inside.

34
00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.770
One binding event now supplies both signals.

35
00:02:58.883 --> 00:03:01.583
That domain lets engineered T cells last.

36
00:03:02.823 --> 00:03:07.723
From natural checkpoints to synthetic receptors, the same wiring rules the response.
